  "verbalization": "Benazepril, a commonly prescribed angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor, is being repurposed for hypertension based on its off-label use in conjunction with other drugs that target hypertension. The evidence suggests that Benazepril, when used off-label, may contribute to the management of hypertension through mechanisms that involve the inhibition of the ACE enzyme, which is central to the renin-angiotensin system. This inhibition leads to the production of angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor that increases blood pressure. The off-label use of Benazepril in combination with other drugs such as Valsartan, Candesartan Cilexetil, and Trandolapril indicates a potential synergistic effect in the treatment of hypertension.\n\nThe integration of these pathways highlights the role of Benazepril in the context of hypertension management, particularly when used in conjunction with other antihypertensive agents. The evidence supports the hypothesis that Benazepril, through its ACE-inhibiting properties, may contribute to lowering blood pressure, although the exact extent of its contribution remains to be fully elucidated. The observed off-label use patterns suggest that Benazepril is being utilized in a context where its therapeutic effects are being leveraged for the management of hypertension, supported by the indications of other drugs that are also used in the treatment of hypertension. This integrated evidence underscores the potential for Benazepril to be considered in the treatment of hypertension, provided the mechanisms and clinical outcomes are further validated.",
